# How to cite WaveChord Synth (GitHub shows it as "Cite this repository"). It is built on the Wavelet Synthesizer (DOI below).
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: "WaveChord Synth"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Guyot"
    given-names: "Pierre Arnaud"
    alias: "OktoPierre"
license: GPL-3.0-or-later
repository-code: "https://github.com/PierreArnaudGUYOT/WaveChord-Synth"
url: "https://pierrearnaudguyot.github.io/WaveChord-Synth/"
version: "beta 0.1"
date-released: "2026-10-18"
references:
  - type: software
    title: "Wavelet Synthesizer"
    authors:
      - family-names: "Guyot"
        given-names: "Pierre Arnaud"
        alias: "OktoPierre"
    doi: 10.5281/zenodo.23431760
    repository-code: "https://github.com/PierreArnaudGUYOT/Free-Wavelet-Synthesizer-VST"
abstract: >-
  WaveChord Synth adds a chord keyboard in the style of electronic chord harps (one octave chooses the chord type, one finger plays the whole chord,
  with voicing, bass, strum and a strum plate) to the Wavelet Synthesizer: a polyphonic synthesizer that shapes sound with the discrete wavelet transform instead of Fourier filters: one cycle
  of each tone is decomposed into 11 octave-spaced wavelet bands and resynthesised band by band. Engine A (wavelet
  subtractive) filters and thins out the atoms of a source wave; engine B (wavelet additive) writes the atoms directly.
  Web application and VST3 / VST2 instrument for Windows x64.
keywords:
  - chord keyboard
  - strum plate
  - sound synthesis
  - discrete wavelet transform
  - synthesizer
  - VST3
  - Web Audio
